Coastal wetlands, such as salt marshes and mangrove forests, act as natural buffers against storm surges and high winds. Their effectiveness relies on two primary physical mechanisms: frictional drag and wave attenuation.

First, the dense vegetation in wetlands creates significant surface roughness. As storm surge waters flow through these plants, the stems and leaves generate friction, slowing the water’s velocity. This reduction in speed decreases the kinetic energy of the surge, meaning that when the water eventually reaches inland infrastructure, it exerts less force and causes less structural damage. Additionally, slower-moving water allows more time for drainage and reduces the likelihood